# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keystone — example environment. Copy to `.env` and edit:
#     cp .env.example .env
# `.env` is gitignored. NEVER commit a real secret; every value below is a
# placeholder and the defaults run the whole system offline with mock providers.
#
# WHAT READS THIS FILE
#   * every `make` target — the Makefile parses this file and EXPORTS it into
#     the recipe's environment, which is the only way the `os.environ`-only
#     variables below and the VITE_* values ever reach a process.
#   * `recon.config.Settings` — reads `<repo>/.env` and `<repo>/service/.env`
#     by absolute path, so `cd service && uv run …` is configured too.
#   Your shell wins over this file in both: `DATABASE_URL=… make migrate`
#   overrides the line below exactly as it reads.
#
# FORMAT. `KEY=value`, one per line. Values are taken verbatim — no quotes, no
# `$VAR` expansion, no inline comments (`FOO=bar # note` sets `bar # note`).
# A line commented out below is a variable the code really reads that has a
# working default; uncomment it only when you want to change that default.
# `service/tests/config/test_env_example_contract.py` fails if this file and
# the code ever drift apart in either direction.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres 16 + pgvector from infra/docker-compose.yml. Host port is 55432
# (not 5432) to avoid colliding with a system Postgres. This single variable
# drives every component — no DSN is ever hardcoded in code.
# The schema, the writer roles, the demo API keys and the budget scopes all
# arrive with `make migrate`; nothing here needs to be created by hand.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://keystone:keystone@localhost:55432/keystone

# OPTIONAL. The **ops** principal's DSN, when the process's own DATABASE_URL is
# not it. `recon.budget` uses it to provision and to lift budget halts, because
# the capped party (`recon_writer`) holds no INSERT on `budget_ledger`. Unset
# falls back to DATABASE_URL, which is right for local development and for the
# sweeper cron, where the configured principal already IS ops.
# OPS_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://keystone:keystone@localhost:55432/keystone

# OPTIONAL, and a SUPPORTED way to supply the connecting role's password:
# libpq reads PGPASSWORD whenever the connection info omits a password, and
# SQLAlchemy's psycopg dialect omits the key entirely when the DSN carries none.
# So `DATABASE_URL=postgresql://recon_writer@host/db` plus PGPASSWORD is
# equivalent to embedding the password — without ever parsing a secret as URL
# syntax. That is not a stylistic preference: `infra/render.yaml` has Render
# generate a **base64** 256-bit password, whose alphabet includes `+`, `/` and
# `=`; pasted into the userinfo component of a DSN, a `@` silently retargets the
# host and a `%` or `/` throws or truncates. The deployed web service therefore
# passes `PGPASSWORD="$RECON_WRITER_PASSWORD"` on its start command and leaves
# DATABASE_URL password-free (which also makes DATABASE_URL safe to print).
# An explicit password in a DSN takes PRECEDENCE over this variable, so the
# review_writer / apply_writer / owner connections — which `recon.db.role_url`
# builds with `URL.set(password=...)`, never through URL text — are unaffected by
# it being set. Local development needs none of this: the local DSN above
# carries its own password and PGPASSWORD is then simply unused.
# PGPASSWORD=

# --- Writer-role passwords ------------------------------------------------
# `make migrate` CREATEs three least-privilege roles and both the migration and
# `recon.db.role_password` read the password from `<ROLE>_PASSWORD`, falling
# back to the role name. The two sides therefore agree by construction, and a
# local run needs none of these set. Production sets all three (see
# infra/render.yaml); if you set one here you must re-run `make migrate`, which
# is what writes the password into Postgres.
# RECON_WRITER_PASSWORD=recon_writer
# REVIEW_WRITER_PASSWORD=review_writer
# APPLY_WRITER_PASSWORD=apply_writer

# --- Health check ---------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Per-probe wall-clock bound for `GET /health`, in seconds. Default
# 2.0 (`recon.config.DEFAULT_HEALTH_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`). Must be > 0; a
# non-positive value is REFUSED at startup rather than accepted, because it
# would make every probe report `timeout` and `/health` answer 503 forever.
#
# RAISE THIS FOR ANY DEPLOY IN FRONT OF A SCALE-TO-ZERO DATABASE. The database
# probe opens a real connection; a suspended Neon compute (default ~5-minute
# idle suspend) takes several seconds to wake, the probe reports `timeout`, and
# `timeout` is fatal — so `/health` answers 503, and a platform that gates the
# deploy on it (`infra/render.yaml` sets `healthCheckPath: /health`, and
# Render's blueprint spec exposes no health-check timeout to widen instead)
# marks the instance unhealthy and never routes traffic to it. 15 is a sane
# value there; local development wants the default.
# HEALTH_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=2.0

# --- Logging / privacy ----------------------------------------------------
# audit_log detail column behaviour.
#   safe = store a hash plus a short preview of prompts/responses (default)
#   full = store raw prompt/response text (local debugging only; never in prod)
LOG_MODE=safe

# --- Request limits --------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. The largest request body `POST /internal/ingest/records` will read,
# in bytes. Enforced DURING the read, not after buffering, so an oversized body
# is abandoned rather than held in memory and then rejected; over the cap the
# endpoint answers 413 with an RFC7807 document that names the limit and never
# echoes the body.
#
# The default is 16 MiB (64 x the 256 KiB per-record cap), and that number is
# measured rather than round: the largest slice the committed manifest expects
# is `fixtures/crm/gen1/contact.jsonl` at 13,052,723 bytes, so a smaller cap
# would refuse a legitimate load. `tests/ingest/test_request_size_bound.py`
# reads both numbers off the committed tree, so a dataset that outgrows the cap
# fails the suite instead of the deploy.
# KEYSTONE_MAX_BODY_BYTES=16777216

# --- Scheduled job triggers ----------------------------------------------
# POST /internal/sync and POST /internal/reconcile are authenticated by the
# header `X-Trigger-Secret`. One secret per job so they can be rotated apart.
# An unset secret FAILS CLOSED (401); it is not an off switch.
# `make sync` sends TRIGGER_SECRET_SYNC from this same file, so the value the
# client presents is by construction the value `make serve` loaded.
# Generate real ones with: openssl rand -hex 32
# Shared secret for the ingestion trigger (POST /internal/sync).
TRIGGER_SECRET_SYNC=replace-me-sync-trigger-secret
# Shared secret for the reconcile trigger (POST /internal/reconcile).
TRIGGER_SECRET_RECONCILE=replace-me-reconcile-trigger-secret
# DEPRECATED single shared secret. Still accepted as a *fallback for sync only*
# (`recon.api.auth.TRIGGER_SECRET_FIELDS`) so a deployment mid-rotation is not
# locked out; never offered to reconcile. Leave it unset on a new install.
# TRIGGER_SECRET=

# --- LLM provider (rationale text only) -----------------------------------
# The LLM never detects conflicts, never computes confidence, never writes.
# Which client the reconciler uses for rationale generation: mock | anthropic
# `mock` is the default and requires no key or network — CI and the graded
# suite run on it so results stay deterministic.
LLM_PROVIDER=mock
# Model id used when LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic. Ignored by the mock provider.
# Use the exact id with no date suffix. It must be priced in the committed
# prices.yaml or the first call fails loudly.
LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# OPTIONAL. Only needed when LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic. Leave blank to stay on
# mock: selecting `anthropic` without a key raises rather than falling back.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Embeddings (pgvector incident similarity) ----------------------------
# Read by `recon.incidents.build_embedding_provider`. Which embedding backend
# to use: mock | voyage | openai. `mock` emits deterministic seeded vectors —
# no network, no key. Selecting a live provider without its key raises; it does
# NOT fall back to the mock, for the reason LLM_PROVIDER does not either.
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=mock
# OPTIONAL. Only needed when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=voyage.
VOYAGE_API_KEY=
# OPTIONAL. Only needed when EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Spend caps (enforced in-app by recon.budget, not by a gateway) -------
# Read straight from the environment by `recon.budget` and by migration 0005,
# which seeds the `daily` and `run:default` ledger rows from them. Changing a
# cap after migrating does NOT rewrite a seeded row; re-provision it as ops.
# Hard ceiling on total LLM spend per UTC day, in US dollars. A run that would
# exceed it halts and writes a `cap_hit` audit row; there is no bypass path.
DAILY_CAP_USD=5.00
# Hard ceiling on a single reconcile run, in US dollars. Reserved worst-case up
# front and settled against provider-reported usage after each call.
PER_RUN_CAP_USD=1.00

# --- Determinism ----------------------------------------------------------
# Master seed for `recon.seed`. Same seed => byte-identical dataset, conflict
# set, and confidence vector. Changing this invalidates golden/ — regenerate.
# `make seed` and `make seed-dev` pass this through as `--seed`, so it is a
# real control and not documentation: unset it and the committed default
# (20260822) applies.
SEED=20260822

# --- Demo API keys --------------------------------------------------------
# Plaintext demo keys for local use; the DB stores only sha256(salt:key).
#
# These are NOT placeholders and must not be edited casually: migration
# 0003_seed_api_clients seeds the hash of exactly these two strings, so any
# other value here is a credential the service will reject. They are throwaway
# demo credentials for a synthetic dataset, deliberately committed so the demo
# is reproducible, and they must never be reused for anything real. Rotating
# them means writing a new migration, not editing this line —
# service/tests/schema/test_env_example_demo_keys.py recomputes the hash from
# the values below and matches it against the seeded row, so the two can never
# drift apart again.
#   client key = tenant-scoped, exists to demonstrate isolation
DEMO_CLIENT_API_KEY=keystone-demo-client-3f7a19c4e2b84d05
#   admin key  = org-wide scope, used by the dashboard for reviewer actions and
#                by the grading suite's own HTTP probe (`recon.suite.probe`)
DEMO_ADMIN_API_KEY=keystone-demo-admin-8c25e0b71a94f36d

# --- Test + grading harness -----------------------------------------------
# A missing DATABASE_URL makes the DB-backed suites SKIP. That once let 76 of
# 81 tests skip while CI reported success, so `KEYSTONE_REQUIRE_DB=1` turns a
# missing database into a hard error. CI sets it; `make test` defaults it to 1.
KEYSTONE_REQUIRE_DB=1

# OPTIONAL. The grading suite's `coverage` row shells out to a real pytest run.
# Point this at a SECOND database so those tests cannot truncate or re-seed the
# database the other fifteen rows are grading. Nothing creates it for you:
#   createdb -h localhost -p 55432 -U keystone ks_coverage
# KEYSTONE_COVERAGE_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://keystone:keystone@localhost:55432/ks_coverage
# OPTIONAL. Seconds before the coverage child is killed and the row goes RED.
# KEYSTONE_COVERAGE_TIMEOUT=2400
# OPTIONAL. Dataset profile the suite's `determinism` row regenerates: full|dev.
# KEYSTONE_SUITE_SEED_PROFILE=full
# OPTIONAL. Scratch database name used by the schema tests.
# KEYSTONE_SCRATCH_DB=keystone_schema_test

# --- Path overrides (all default to the committed trees) ------------------
# ALL FOUR MUST BE ABSOLUTE. The code resolves a relative value against the
# PROCESS working directory, which is `service/` for every entry point (the
# Makefile runs `uv --directory service`), not the repository root — so a
# relative path here points one level deeper than it reads.
#
# OPTIONAL. Where the JSONL adapters read snapshots from. Defaults to
# `<repo>/fixtures`, which `make seed` writes. Point it at the inner-loop
# dataset `make seed-dev` produces to work against ~6k records instead of 120k.
# KEYSTONE_FIXTURES_DIR=/abs/path/to/keystone/.scratch/seed-dev/fixtures
# OPTIONAL. Where the committed grading contract is read from. Default `<repo>/golden`.
# KEYSTONE_GOLDEN_DIR=/abs/path/to/keystone/golden
# OPTIONAL. Where the versioned invariant SQL is read from. Default `<repo>/rules`.
# KEYSTONE_RULES_DIR=/abs/path/to/keystone/rules
# OPTIONAL. Where `recon.suite` writes scorecard.txt. Default `<repo>/docs`.
# KEYSTONE_SCORECARD_DIR=/abs/path/to/keystone/docs

# --- CORS -----------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Comma-separated browser origins the API answers, read by
# `recon.app.allowed_origins` straight from the process environment (the name is
# `recon.app.CORS_ORIGINS_ENV`; it is deployment wiring, not a `Settings`
# credential — same as the other KEYSTONE_* overrides). Values are trimmed of
# whitespace, order is preserved and duplicates are dropped. Unset or blank
# means the committed default, http://localhost:5173 and http://127.0.0.1:5173 —
# the Vite dev server — so `make dash` against `make serve` needs nothing here.
# Credentials are never enabled, whatever this says. `*` is honoured only when
# an operator sets it deliberately; it is not the default.
#
# EACH ENTRY MUST BE A FULL ORIGIN: `scheme://host[:port]`, WITH the scheme and
# WITHOUT a trailing slash or path. Starlette compares the browser's `Origin`
# header to this list by exact string equality (`origin in self.allow_origins`),
# and a browser sends `https://keystone-dashboard.onrender.com` — never with a
# trailing slash. So `keystone-dashboard.onrender.com` (no scheme) and
# `https://keystone-dashboard.onrender.com/` (trailing slash) both match
# nothing, and the symptom is every dashboard request failing with an opaque
# browser CORS error and NOTHING in the service log, because the browser refuses
# the request before it is sent. The deployed value is therefore written out in
# full — `infra/render.yaml` sets it to `https://keystone-dashboard.onrender.com`
# for exactly this reason (Render's `property: host` yields a bare hostname,
# which is why the scheme is composed rather than referenced).
# KEYSTONE_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# --- Budget scope override (ops only) -------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL. Redirects the mandated daily cap at a stand-in ledger row so a
# burst test does not spend the day's real budget to prove the day's budget
# works. Every use is logged loudly. Production leaves it unset.
# KEYSTONE_DAILY_SCOPE=daily-scratch

# --- Dashboard (Vite) -----------------------------------------------------
# Vite inlines any VITE_-prefixed value into the client bundle at build time.
# Anything here is PUBLIC. Never put a real secret in a VITE_ variable.
#
# PRECEDENCE. `make dash` exports these into the Vite process, and Vite ranks
# process environment ABOVE `dashboard/.env.local`. So with `make dash` this
# file wins; run `pnpm --dir dashboard dev` directly to use
# `dashboard/.env.local` on its own. The two ship identical values.
# Base URL of the Keystone service the dashboard talks to.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000
# API key the dashboard sends. Must equal DEMO_ADMIN_API_KEY above — the
# dashboard performs reviewer actions, which need the admin scope.
VITE_API_KEY=keystone-demo-admin-8c25e0b71a94f36d
# OPTIONAL. Set to 1 to render against the in-browser mock service instead of
# the real API — the zero-setup demo path, with a visible banner. `pnpm dev:mock`
# sets it for you. Leave it unset for the real client.
# VITE_USE_MOCK_API=1
